WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.000
A neuron has no label saying where it came from.

2
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.880
So how do you find out?

3
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:12.480
A birth date marks one cohort of cells.

4
00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.860
A lineage is one family.

5
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.700
To follow one, write a mark into a cell's own DNA.

6
00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.520
Then every descendant carries it.

7
00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:27.100
The tool that does this is an enzyme called Cre recombinase.

8
00:00:28.033 --> 00:00:31.013
Start with the gene for a fluorescent protein.

9
00:00:31.693 --> 00:00:34.333
A reporter like this shows where a cell is.

10
00:00:35.093 --> 00:00:38.033
In front of it, place a transcriptional stop signal.

11
00:00:38.793 --> 00:00:41.673
Put a loxP site on each side of that stop.

12
00:00:42.693 --> 00:00:46.573
Each site is a short DNA sequence, 34 base pairs long.

13
00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:49.513
This is a Cre-dependent reporter.

14
00:00:50.153 --> 00:00:52.873
While the stop is in place, the gene is not read.

15
00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:57.167
Now Cre recombinase appears in a cell.

16
00:00:57.807 --> 00:01:00.087
It deletes the stop that the sites flank.

17
00:01:00.867 --> 00:01:03.427
The reporter is read, and the cell glows.

18
00:01:04.747 --> 00:01:08.067
Cre is made from a promoter active in one cell type.

19
00:01:08.927 --> 00:01:11.727
Only cells that make Cre delete the stop.

20
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:13.667
Their neighbors stay dark.

21
00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.190
Now that cell divides.

22
00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:21.170
The deletion is in its DNA, so both daughters inherit it.

23
00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:26.570
The reporter is read in that cell and in every one of its descendants from then on.

24
00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:28.430
The marking is permanent.

25
00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:32.230
That is exactly what lineage tracing requires.

26
00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:35.973
Cre can also remove a gene.

27
00:01:36.993 --> 00:01:40.033
Flank an important part of a gene with loxP sites.

28
00:01:40.853 --> 00:01:43.933
That allele is called floxed, and it works normally.

29
00:01:45.173 --> 00:01:50.293
Supply Cre from a promoter active in one cell type, and the gene is deleted there

30
00:01:50.293 --> 00:01:55.133
alone. A whole-body knockout cannot tell you which tissue mattered.

31
00:01:56.233 --> 00:01:59.833
For timing, fuse Cre to a modified estrogen receptor.

32
00:01:59.833 --> 00:02:03.333
Then the deletion waits for the drug tamoxifen.

33
00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:08.947
One trap. A reporter cross tells you where Cre acted.

34
00:02:09.427 --> 00:02:12.307
It does not tell you where the floxed gene was lost.

35
00:02:13.247 --> 00:02:16.587
The two lox-flanked targets recombine independently.

36
00:02:17.847 --> 00:02:19.827
Drivers can be leaky or mosaic.

37
00:02:21.087 --> 00:02:22.827
Protein made before the cut persists.

38
00:02:23.507 --> 00:02:25.227
So measure the loss itself.

39
00:02:26.107 --> 00:02:29.967
A reporter shows where Cre acted, not where the gene is gone.
